--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b8c5f8bea1b438d4687ad9b712e77c3e, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level 1-15 After Battle Cutscene
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CutsceneData
  line:
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: "An hour or so after the shooting a dead-wagon from San Juan de Di\xF3s
      Hospital took Rizal\u2019s body to Paco Cemetery. No visitors were admitted
      to the cemetery while the interment was going on, and for several weeks after
      guards watched over the grave, lest Filipinos might come by night to steal
      away the body and apportion the clothing among themselves as relics of a martyr."
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Even the exact spot of the interment was intended to be unknown, but
      friends of the family were among the attendants at the burial and dropped into
      the grave a marble slab which had been furnished them, bearing the initials
      of the full baptismal name, Jose Protasio Rizal, in reversed order.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Before his execution, he wrote letters to his close friend Ferdinand
      Blumentritt, saying that when he received that letter, he would be dead by
      then. He also asks him to never think ill of him. A letter was also addressed
      to Paciano, stating that his one and only brother, suffered for him greatly.
      He helped him in many ways and that he is thankful for it. Lastly, he says
      that he dies not a traitorous death.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: A poem was left in an alcohol lamp, containing his poem "Mi Ultimo
      Adios. It was found by a Dapitan schoolboy who heard that there was something
      inside. The boy hastily copied the contents and made mistakes in the process.
      It was then mailed it to Mr. Basa in Hong Kong.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Eventually, Narcisa Rizal found his body and acquired it after the
      treaty of Paris. Only then were the Rizals able to feel relief that the body
      of their kin was found.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: The following subsequent events after Rizal's execution became a reminder
      of Rizal's service to the Philippines, and what he has done for it to thrive.
      December 30 is now a reminder for a friend, a martyr, and an absolute hero
      who loved his country, the Philippines.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: The End.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Narrator
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Jose Rizal Campaign. Complete.
    isLeftSide: 0
    pose: 0
